
============================================================================================
1  THE BASELINE, ITS PARAMETER SENSITIVITIES, SYMBOLICALLY
============================================================================================
  M(Y), C, K written from gantrySystemExtended.m with the absorber absent.
  The ten identifiable combinations enter exactly one of the three matrices each,
  which Sect. 8.2 measured to be exact (bit-exact zeros on the other two).

  dM/d(m_diff), the X-Theta mass coupling. This is the direction the absorber
  offset ma*L0 corrupts, and the one carrying 98.8 percent of the measured bias:
⎡     L_b   ⎤
⎢ 0   ───  0⎥
⎢      2    ⎥
⎢           ⎥
⎢L_b        ⎥
⎢───   0   0⎥
⎢ 2         ⎥
⎢           ⎥
⎣ 0    0   0⎦

============================================================================================
2  THE ADDITION, AND THE CONDITION, IN FORMULAS
============================================================================================
  f_a = -( M_a qdd + C_a qd + K_a q ),  M_a, C_a, K_a symmetric, 18 unknowns:
    M_a = a00, a01, a02, a11, a12, a22
    C_a = b00, b01, b02, b11, b12, b22
    K_a = c00, c01, c02, c11, c12, c22

  Moments of the operating domain: S0 = E[q q^T], S1 = E[q' q'^T],
  S2 = E[q'' q''^T], and the stationarity identities
      E[q q'^T], E[q' q''^T] antisymmetric,    E[q q''^T] = -S1
  which are Sect. 8.4's integration-by-parts identities in expectation form.

  Ten conditions built. Each is LINEAR in the eighteen unknowns, as claimed:
    coefficient matrix is (10, 18)

============================================================================================
3  WHAT COUPLES TO WHAT, READ OFF THE FORMULAS
============================================================================================
  With all nine terms carried, the conditions do NOT decouple. An earlier
  version reported the damping conditions involve C_a alone; that was an
  artefact of dropping the cross-parity terms and is retracted.
  Unknowns actually appearing in each damping condition:
    damp cg1     unknowns: ['a00', 'a01', 'a02', 'a11', 'a12', 'a22', 'b00', 'b01', 'b02', 'b11', 'b12', 'b22', 'c00', 'c01', 'c02', 'c11', 'c12', 'c22']
    damp cg2     unknowns: ['a00', 'a01', 'a02', 'a11', 'a12', 'a22', 'b00', 'b01', 'b02', 'b11', 'b12', 'b22', 'c00', 'c01', 'c02', 'c11', 'c12', 'c22']
    damp cy      unknowns: ['a00', 'a01', 'a02', 'a11', 'a12', 'b00', 'b01', 'b02', 'b11', 'b12', 'b22', 'c00', 'c01', 'c02', 'c11', 'c12']
    damp cb_sum  unknowns: ['a00', 'a01', 'a02', 'a12', 'a22', 'b00', 'b01', 'b02', 'b11', 'b12', 'b22', 'c00', 'c01', 'c02', 'c12', 'c22']

  The cross-parity terms are weighted by the antisymmetric cross-moments T01
  and T12, so they are small when the domain is near stationary and the matrix
  products are near symmetric, but they are NOT zero. The whole 10 x 18 system
  must be solved together.

============================================================================================
4  SOLVE, with the coefficients evaluated over the operating domain
============================================================================================
  The FORMULA is the symbolic one above. Only its ten times eighteen COEFFICIENTS
  describe where the machine operates, and they are evaluated as domain averages.
  Two things must be consistent with the empirical condition or the derived addition
  will not transfer, and both were wrong in a first attempt:
    (a) W = M(Y)^-T D M(Y)^-1 depends on the SCHEDULING VARIABLE, so it belongs
        INSIDE the average. Freezing it at one Y left rho* at 5.0e-02.
    (b) the average must run over the SAME tuples the reference set uses, not a
        differently trimmed window of the same records.
