============================================================================================
5  A PHYSICALLY MEANINGFUL MEMBER
============================================================================================
  Pick the member of the class that is purely REACTIVE (C_a = 0, forced anyway by the
  lossless requirement) and has the smallest Frobenius norm for a fixed size. Solve
  the damping subsystem first: C_a = 0 satisfies it identically, which is the
  formula-level statement of why the addition must be lossless.

  lossless subsystem: 10 x 12 in (M_a, K_a), rank 10, so the LOSSLESS admissible
  class has dimension 2. C_a = 0 is imposed, not hoped for.

  M_a =
[[ 0.0005  0.0004 -0.004 ]
 [ 0.0004 -0.      0.    ]
 [-0.004   0.     -0.0002]]
  K_a =
[[ 0.88    0.0708 -0.4521]
 [ 0.0708 -0.0627  0.0399]
 [-0.4521  0.0399 -0.1037]]

  residual ||A v|| / ||A|| = 2.666e-20   (machine zero means orthogonal by construction)

============================================================================================
6  CHECK THE DERIVED FORMULA AGAINST THE EMPIRICAL CONDITION, Eq. (17)
============================================================================================
  The derivation targets Eq. (16), an integral against the declared domain
  measure. Training happens on a finite record, which is Eq. (17). The gap
  between them is sampling error, and it is exactly what Plumlee and Joseph
  criticise the prior art for ignoring. Measuring it is not optional.

